  "complex": {
    "id": "kinetempo-neck-desk-block",
    "name": "Neck: isometrics, chin tucks and stretches",
    "description": "About eight minutes, once a day, for the ordinary stiff neck of desk work: isometric holds in four directions, then chin tucks for the deep neck flexors, then a stretch for the upper trapezius and levator on each side.\n\nNone of it should hurt. The isometric holds are gentle — you push into your own hand and nothing moves; a third of your strength is plenty. The chin tuck is a small, precise movement: the chin slides straight back as if making a double chin, the head does not nod down. Stretches go to the first firm resistance, not into pain.\n\nGet assessed before starting if the neck pain came from a fall or a whiplash, or if it comes with numbness, pins and needles or weakness in an arm, dizziness, or headaches that are new and severe. Not medical advice.",
    "restBetweenMs": 30000,
